 this part  on typography we're going to create a  controller to make sure our text can be  scaled and moved around without issue  first let's create a null layer  and rename it to text control  you can then select all the layers below  it and parent it to that node if you  want to scale the text you can press s  for scale and scale it up or down as you  wish now let's get into the next step  which is creating the background I went  ahead and pre-comp to our text layer  moving all attributes into that com to  create our background I'll first make a  solid layer add a fill to it and pick a  dark color then let's bring in our  background imagery the Drone shot we saw  earlier and pick a frame we like right  click on the footage go to time Freeze  Frame we now have an image we can work  with for our background then I'm gonna  mask out the top of the image and  feather it by around 400 once we're  happy with this we can tint the footage  black and white  and play with the levels to adjust its  contrast this being too overpowering  let's lower its opacity to 15  I like this once we're happy with this  we can bring in our texture lab image in  between the footage and the solid tint  it and adjust levels you can always play  with the scale as there are very high  resolution texture we now have some  interesting typography with texture in  it a cool background to work with let's  go ahead and prepare our comp for the  next step I went ahead and applied a  tritone adjustment layer and picked off  some colors from the movie poster we saw  earlier to create the yellow text for  the word November I duplicated the text  comp masked out the word and applied the  tint to it with the color yellow now  let's get into the next step which is  adding atmosphere when it comes to  Atmosphere I think experimenting is a  key word first let's try to see what it  would look like with our fog footage  let's drag it below our tritone effect  and composite it with the shift Channel  effect take the Alpha from the luminance  and scale the layer way up I like this  but it's a little bit too much so let's  lower the opacity to 50 this is good  then let's add some particles let's take  the footage here and drag it below our  Triton effect over the fog let's solo it  and fit it to our comp with as this is  4K footage as with all of the other  textures within this tutorial I love to  add a levels effect to really dial in  the look to my liking either adding more  or adding less  it's up to you it doesn't need to be  much it doesn't need to be perfect keep  in mind you can always combine particle  footage flip it offset it scale it and  so on let's set this particle layer to  screen mode I went ahead and imported  our prism footage into our comp below  the tritone effect scaled it and found a  nice spot for it for it to appear and  leave our composition this being too  much I'm gonna lower its opacity to 5  for the look I went ahead and created an  adjustment layer added the levels effect  on it tweak the values added the lumetri  effect on it and set the faded film to  10. one last thing we're going to do  before moving on to our next and final  step is to create a displacement map for  our text in this new composition I  imported our grunge foil texture and  blurred it by 5 pixels back in our main  comp we're going to import our  displacement map put it at the bottom  below our background layer and hide it  on the main text layer we can load the  displacement map effect and choose our  displacement map comp I set the max  horizontal displacement to 8 and the  vertical displacement to zero we have  this weird effect on the text because we  have two iterations of our text comp  let's select the displacement map effect  press Ctrl alt C to copy the effect and  its properties and Ctrl V on the second  iteration we now have some subtle  displacement to our type now let's get  into the next step which is setting up  the camera before setting up the camera  let's take all of the layers we want  animated and make them 3D then you can  proceed to create a camera 35 millimeter  works well and what I like to do is to  create an object pairing the camera to  it make the null object 3D and separate  its Dimension then I'm gonna go ahead  and select our background imagery our  texture lives texture and our solid  press P for prison position and offset  the Z position to maybe 500. this  creates some nice Parallax to animate  the camera go to the beginning of your  timeline and press the stopwatch to set  a keyframe then go to the end of your  timeline and set this to maybe 100 maybe  50. we now have a very slow push in with  the camera and some nice Parallax to  take this a step further we can offset  how the text appears in our text  pre-comp let's select our first layer  offset it by 12 frames select our second  text layer and offset it to just about a  second we now have some nice offset and  animation to our text we can also create  an animator for the November text by  selecting tracking and setting a  keyframe for the tracking amount at the  beginning and at the end but keep this  very subtle maybe to end drag the first  keyframe before the text starts this  gives us a nice nice subtle animation  before wrapping it up let's go over a  few adjustment layers that take the  digital feel out of your composition  first grain I selected the preset took  down the intensity and the size and set  the color to monochromatic  then chromatic aberration here I'm using  the borrow suit prism plugin but feel  free to use Quick chromatic aberration  too as it's a free plugin I set the  starting depth to 0.99 and the ending  depth to  1.001 this creates some nice chromatic  aberration around the edges of our text  our last adjustment layer is simply a  blur set to 0.2 with two iterations to  take out the aliasing in our text it's  very subtle but it makes a difference  now let's wrap up this lesson by going  over the steps one more time first we  went on Pinterest and gathered some  inspiration for our project we then went  on texture labs to gather some textures  and pixels for footage once our assets  were imported into After Effects we  created some text and added texture  within it we then created a background  using a solid some texture and a  background image then we stylized our  text and composition position using a  Triton effect and some color once we  were happy with this we then added  atmosphere through a layer of fog some  lens flare and some particles after  applying the look we then created the  camera made our layers 3D and created  some Parallax to tie everything together  we then added some grain some chromatic  aberration and some blur let's have a  look at what we've made in full screen  I hope you've enjoyed this lesson as  much as I love teaching it if you'd like  to see more content like this leave a  comment below

